"Solutions for Advent of Code 2025, Day 09.

Part 1: Find the largest axis-aligned rectangle using two red tiles as
opposite corners. Area = (|x1-x2|+1) * (|y1-y2|+1).

Part 2: Red tiles form a rectilinear polygon whose interior is green.
Find the largest rectangle using two red tiles as corners that fits
entirely within the red/green region.

Approach: Part 1 is brute-force over all tile pairs via `tails` and
`foldl` to find the maximum area without intermediate lists. Part 2
compresses the coordinates to their ranks (about 250 by 250 cells), marks
which cells are inside the polygon by scanning each row across its vertical
edges, builds 2D prefix sums with an `arr` array, and then tests any
candidate rectangle in constant time. A pair is only tested when its area
beats the best so far.

Notable eucalypt techniques:
- `dict` for coordinate ranks, `set` for edge crossings, `arr` for prefix sums
- `scanl` for the inside/outside parity scan and the row accumulation
- `foldl-strict` with a lazy `fits` argument so the polygon test runs only
  when the area wins
- list destructuring in function parameters (extra elements are ignored, so
  `[x, y, column, row]` points work with `area([x1, y1], [x2, y2])`)
- `par-max` over per-suffix maxima

Running time: ~1.5s sequential / ~1.1s parallel (part 1), ~7.8s sequential / ~6s parallel (part 2), on 4 cores
"

parse-coord(line): line str.split-on(",") map(num)

area([x1, y1], [x2, y2]): (abs(x1 - x2) + 1) * (abs(y1 - y2) + 1)

` "Max area from head of a list to any later element."
best-from(ps): ps tail map(area(ps head)) max-of

` "Max area over all distinct pairs of points.
Each suffix's best area is independent of the others, so the per-suffix
maxima run under `par-max` (same value as `map(best-from) max-of`;
parallel on macOS/Linux)."
best-area(pts): {
  tls: tails(pts) filter(count ; (> 1))
}.(tls par-max(best-from))

parse(data): data filter(!= "") map(parse-coord)

solve(pts): best-area(pts)

` { target: :test
    import: "ex1=inputs/day09-ex1.txt" }
test: {
  part-1: ex1 parse solve //= 50
  RESULT: part-1 then(:PASS, :FAIL)
}

` "Rank of each distinct value among the sorted distinct values, as a dict."
ranks(vals): dict.of(zip(vals sort-nums uniq, ℕ))

` "Vertical edges of the polygon through `pts`, as `[x, y-lo, y-hi]`."
vertical-edges(pts): {
  vertical?[p, q]: first(p) = first(q)
  vert-edge[p, q]: [first(p), min(second(p), second(q)), max(second(p), second(q))]
}.(zip(pts, pts rotate(1)) filter(vertical?) map(vert-edge))

` "Does the vertical edge `[x, y-lo, y-hi]` span the band starting at row `j`?"
spans?(j, [_, y-lo, y-hi]): y-lo <= j ∧ j < y-hi

` "Flip the inside/outside state `p` when cell column `i` is crossed."
toggle(xs, p, i): (i ∈ xs) then(1 - p, p)

` "Inside-ness (1 or 0) of each of the `w` cells across the band at row `j`:
the state flips each time the scan crosses a vertical edge spanning the band."
inside-row(w, edges, j): {
  crossings: edges filter(spans?(j)) map(first) set.of
}.(range(0, w) scanl(toggle(crossings), 0) tail)

` "2D prefix sums over the `[rows, cols]` grid of cells: entry `[j, i]` counts
the cells above row `j` and left of column `i` that are inside."
inside-sums(w, edges, h): {
  rows: range(0, h) map(inside-row(w, edges)) map(scanl(+, 0))
  zero-row: repeat(0) take(w + 1)
}.(arr.from-flat([h + 1, w + 1], rows scanl(zip-with(+), zero-row) concat))

` "Is every cell of the compressed rectangle between corners `p` and `q`
(each `[x, y, column, row]`) inside the polygon? True when its prefix-sum
total equals its cell count."
filled?(sums, [_, _, ia, ja], [_, _, ib, jb]): {
  i1: min(ia, ib)
  i2: max(ia, ib)
  j1: min(ja, jb)
  j2: max(ja, jb)
  total: arr.get([j2, i2], sums) - arr.get([j1, i2], sums) - arr.get([j2, i1], sums) + arr.get([j1, i1], sums)
}.(total = (i2 - i1) * (j2 - j1))

` "Keep the larger of `best` and area `a`, but only if `fits` (the rectangle
lies inside the polygon). `fits` is lazy, so it is only tested when `a` wins."
upgrade(fits, a, best): (a > best ∧ fits) then(a, best)

upgrade-with(ok?, p, best, q): upgrade(ok?(p, q), area(p, q), best)

` "Largest filled rectangle from the head `p` of `ps` to a later point."
best-filled-from(ok?, [p : qs]): qs foldl-strict(upgrade-with(ok?, p), 0)

` "Tile `[x, y]` extended with the ranks of its coordinates."
rank-point(rx, ry, [x, y]): [x, y, rx dict.get(x), ry dict.get(y)]

` "Part 2 on the red tiles `pts`. Coordinates are compressed to their ranks,
so the polygon is a grid of at most `n/2` by `n/2` cells; prefix sums over
it test any candidate rectangle in constant time, and a pair is only tested
when its area beats the best so far. Each suffix's best is independent, so
the maxima run under `par-max`."
solve2(pts): {
  rx: pts map(first) ranks
  ry: pts map(second) ranks
  edges: vertical-edges(pts) map(compress(rx, ry))
  w: (rx dict.size) - 1
  h: (ry dict.size) - 1
  sums: inside-sums(w, edges, h)
  tls: pts map(rank-point(rx, ry)) tails filter(count ; (> 1))
}.(tls par-max(best-filled-from(filled?(sums))))

` "Replace an edge's raw coordinates by their ranks."
compress(rx, ry, [x, y-lo, y-hi]): [rx dict.get(x), ry dict.get(y-lo), ry dict.get(y-hi)]

` { target: :test2
    import: "ex1=inputs/day09-ex1.txt" }
test2: {
  part-2: ex1 parse solve2 //= 24
  RESULT: part-2 then(:PASS, :FAIL)
}
